{-
   Narrowing out of a 64-bit type, which on JavaScript means out of the `bigint` domain.

   `truncate(w)` from a `U64` is "the low thirty-two bits", and this target used to emit
   `Number(w) >>> 0` for it - which is not that. `Number` converts the *whole* magnitude to a double
   first, so every bit above the fifty-third is gone by the time `>>> 0` asks for the low
   thirty-two: `truncate(0x0123456789ABCDEF)` answered 0x89ABCE00 where both the specification and
   the native build say 0x89ABCDEF. See `narrowedFromLong` in codegen/js/inst.cpp, which reduces on
   the `bigint` and only then converts.

   **Every value here goes through a function**, and that is the whole design of this fixture. A
   narrowing of a *constant* is folded in `compiler/opt`, in full precision, so the optimized build
   was right and only the unoptimized one was wrong - which is exactly the shape the fixture
   runner's optimizer-equivalence check exists to catch and is how this was found. Inlining is what
   would put the constant back, so the values are parameters and the checks are comparisons rather
   than a golden.

   The three widths below are the three cases: a result whose top bit is clear, one whose top bit is
   set, and one that fills the word. Only the first survives the old lowering, which is why a
   digest's high half looked fine and its low half did not.
-}

import Native

fn lowWord(w: U64) -> U32 = truncate(w)
fn highWord(w: U64) -> U32 = truncate(w `shr` 32)
fn lowByte(w: U64) -> U8 = truncate(w)
fn lowSigned(w: U64) -> Int = truncate(w)
fn asInt(x: U32) -> Int = bitcast(x)

fn main() -> Int:
    -- Above 2^53, so the double `Number` produces is already inexact.
    let w = 0x0123456789ABCDEF :: U64

    if lowWord(w) != (0x89ABCDEF :: U32): return 1
    if highWord(w) != (0x01234567 :: U32): return 2
    if lowByte(w) != (0xEF :: U8): return 3

    -- The signed target, which reduces at the same width and reads the bits back as `Int`.
    if lowSigned(w) != asInt(0x89ABCDEF): return 4

    -- A high half whose own top bit is set, which is the case `highWord` above does not cover.
    let v = 0xFEDCBA9876543210 :: U64
    if lowWord(v) != (0x76543210 :: U32): return 5
    if highWord(v) != (0xFEDCBA98 :: U32): return 6
    if lowByte(v) != (0x10 :: U8): return 7

    -- Every bit set, so no reduction anywhere may leave a sign behind.
    let full = 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF :: U64
    if lowWord(full) != (0xFFFFFFFF :: U32): return 8
    if highWord(full) != (0xFFFFFFFF :: U32): return 9

    return 0
